NNAAMMEE
       pnmsmooth - smooth out an image


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppnnmmssmmooootthh [--wwiiddtthh==_c_o_l_s] [--hheeiigghhtt==_r_o_w_s] [_p_n_m_f_i_l_e] [--ssiizzee]

       Minimum  unique  abbreviations  of  options is acceptable.  You may use
       double hyphens instead of single hyphen to denote options.  You may use
       white space in place of the equals sign to separate an option name from
       its value.


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmssmmooootthh smoothes out an image by replacing each pixel with the  aver‐
       age  of  its  width  X height neighbors.  It is implemented as a progam
       that invokes ppnnmmccoonnvvooll with an appropriate convolution matrix.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppnnmmssmmooootthh recognizes the
       following command line options:



       --wwiiddtthh==_c_o_l_s

       --hheeiigghhtt==_r_o_w_s
              These options specify the dimensions of the convolution  matrix.
              Default dimensions are 3 wide and 3 high.

              Before  Netpbm  10.49  (December  2009), the maximum size of the
              convolution matrix is limited by the maxval of  the  image  such
              that  width  *  height  *  2  must  not exceed the maxval.  (use
              ppaammddeepptthh to increase the maxval if necessary).

              These options were new in Netpbm 10.32 (February 2006).   Before
              that, use --ssiizzee.


       --ssiizzee  This  deprecated  option  exists  in current Netpbm for backward
              compatibility.  It was obsoleted by --wwiiddtthh and --hheeiigghhtt in Netpbm
              10.32 (February 2006).

              When  you  use  this option, the first two program arguments are
              the width and height, respectively, of  the  convolution  matrix
              and  do  the same thing as the --wwiiddtthh and --hheeiigghhtt option values.
              The third (optional) program argument is the input file name.

              In reality, in old ppnnmmssmmooootthh, the width and height are two  val‐
              ues  of  the  --ssiizzee option, but the modern Netpbm command syntax
              paradigm doesn't allow an option with multiple  values,  so  in‐
              stead  --ssiizzee is an option with no value and width and height are
              program arguments.  That has the fortunate effect of making  the
              following  command  mean the same in current ppnnmmssmmooootthh as in old
              ppnnmmssmmooootthh:
                   pnmsmooth -size 5 5 infile.ppm >outfile.ppm



       --dduummpp==_d_u_m_p_f_i_l_e
              This options makes ppnnmmssmmooootthh only show you the  convolution  ma‐
              trix.   It  writes to Standard Output a ppnnmmccoonnvvooll --mmaattrriixx option
              value that represents the matrix.  It does not invoke  ppnnmmccoonnvvooll
              and does not produce an output image.

              Before  Netpbm 10.49 (December 2009), this option is rather dif‐
              ferent.  It takes a file name as a value, and it writes to  that
              file  the  convolution  matrix  as a PGM file (as used to be the
              normal input for ppnnmmccoonnvvooll).






SSEEEE AALLSSOO
       ppnnmmccoonnvvooll(1), ppnnmm(1)


HHIISSTTOORRYY
       Before Netpbm 10.32 (February 2006), ppnnmmssmmooootthh did not use  the  modern
       Netpbm  command line parser, so had an unconventional command line syn‐
       tax.  Most importantly, you could not use an equal sign or  double  hy‐
       phens in the options.

       Before  Netpbm  10.49  (December 2009), there was a --dduummpp option.  This
       strange option caused ppnnmmssmmooootthh not to do any smoothing or produce  any
       output  image  but  instead  write the convolution matrix it would have
       used, as PGM file such as ppnnmmccoonnvvooll used to use, to a file you specify.
       The idea was you could then use that file with a separate invocation of
       ppnnmmccoonnvvooll.

       Then, in Netpbm 10.49, there was a rather different --dduummpp option with a
       similar  purpose:  It  caused  ppnnmmssmmooootthh  to  write to Standard Error a
       string suitable as a value for the ppnnmmccoonnvvooll --mmaattrriixx option (an  option
       that was new in Netpbm 10.49).

       But in Netpbm 10.51 (June 2010), ppnnmmccoonnvvooll started using the even newer
       ppnnmmccoonnvvooll --nnoorrmmaalliizzee option (new in 10.50), which made  specifying  the
       convolution  matrix for the kind of smoothing that ppnnmmssmmooootthh does triv‐
       ial, so --dduummpp disappeared from ppnnmmssmmooootthh.

       (There were also ease of implementation issues that kept us from simply
       keeping  the  original  --dduummpp  around for backward compatibility: As we
       modified ppnnmmssmmooootthh to take advantage of the new features of  ppnnmmccoonnvvooll,
       which  ppnnmmssmmooootthh  uses  internally, the information needed to implement
       --dduummpp was no longer available in the program).

